/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  petroleumCuts01_riazi_table36_tbp
  =================================

  CUT a TRUE-BOILING-POINT curve into PSEUDO-COMPONENTS.  A crude fraction
  is a continuum of species; a process model needs a handful of lumps.  The
  op `petroleumCuts` divides the curve into cuts, takes each cut's Tb at the
  mid-point of its distilled interval, gives it a specific gravity, and hands
  the pair (Tb, SG) to the SAME Riazi-Daubert estimator `estimateComponent`
  uses (MW, Tc, Pc, Vc; omega by Lee-Kesler; Cp by Kesler-Lee).  One ESTIMATE
  record per cut is written into THIS case (constant/components/
  petCut_NN.estimated.dat, git-ignored: a proposal you review and promote
  with `mv`, never a standard record).

  THE CURVE AND WHAT IS -- AND IS NOT -- VALIDATED.  The six TBP points are
  the naphtha-kerosene blend printed in Riazi, "Characterization and
  Properties of Petroleum Fractions" (ASTM MNL50, 2005), Table 3.6, p. 103
  (a TBP column, vol% distilled vs C, quoted there from the API Technical
  Data Book).  The book prints NO cut Tb, cut SG, MW or Tc for it: it uses
  the curve to illustrate the D86/TBP/EFV interconversions (Example 3.2),
  and there it estimates the blend's SG as 0.7862 from Eq. 3.17 -- the one
  printed number this case can reproduce, and does (feed SG 0.7862 from
  `specificGravityFrom TBP`).  EVERYTHING ELSE IS ILLUSTRATIVE: the cut
  boundaries are this case's choice, and no number is validated against a
  distillation unit or a measured cut.  The ASTM D86 and EFV columns of the
  same table are NOT used (the op refuses D86 input: the conversion is not
  implemented).

  HOW EACH CUT IS CHARACTERISED (all equations are Riazi's, by number):
    * Tb of a cut = the TBP temperature at the mid-point of its distilled
      interval (p. 83, footnote a: a mid-point temperature can serve as Tb
      for a narrow fraction), piecewise-linear between the curve points.
    * SG of a cut = (1.8 Tb)^(1/3) / Kw with ONE Watson factor for the whole
      feed (Eq. 2.13 rearranged).  THIS IS AN ASSUMPTION OF THE OP, NOT A
      RECOMMENDATION OF THE BOOK; `method declared;` lets you give the SG of
      each cut instead.  Kw is Eq. 2.13 at the feed SG and the feed's MeABP
      (Eq. 3.3-3.5), solved by fixed point.
    * API gravity (Eq. 2.4) and Watson K (Eq. 2.13) are published per cut
      and for the blend.
    * CLOSURE: the cuts re-blended by volume (Eq. 3.45) must reproduce the
      declared feed SG within the DECLARED tolerance.  A miss is announced,
      never adjusted.  Both ops below re-blend about 1 % above the feed SG
      (the curve is convex and the mid-point Tb is not the cut's mean); op
      `cuts5` declares 2 % and closes, op `cuts3` declares 0.5 % on purpose
      and prints the announcement.  Note that the miss does NOT shrink
      monotonically with the number of cuts.

  SEE: the cut table, the blend line, the closure line, the records.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

operations
(
    {
        name        cuts5;
        type        petroleumCuts;

        curve
        {
            type    TBP;
            basis   volume;                  // the book's TBP is vol% distilled
            points
            (
                { fraction 0.00;  T 10.0  C; }
                { fraction 0.10;  T 71.1  C; }
                { fraction 0.30;  T 143.3 C; }
                { fraction 0.50;  T 204.4 C; }
                { fraction 0.70;  T 250.6 C; }
                { fraction 0.90;  T 291.7 C; }
            );
        }

        feed    { specificGravityFrom TBP; }     // Riazi Eq. 3.17, TBP row of Table 3.4

        cuts    { boundaries ( 0.0 0.1 0.3 0.5 0.7 1.0 ); }

        specificGravity { method constantWatsonK; }

        closure { specificGravityTolerance 0.02; }    // 2 % on SG, the author's choice

        output  { proposal auto; prefix petCut; file cuts5.csv; }
    }

    {
        name        cuts3;
        type        petroleumCuts;

        curve
        {
            type    TBP;
            basis   volume;
            points
            (
                { fraction 0.00;  T 10.0  C; }
                { fraction 0.10;  T 71.1  C; }
                { fraction 0.30;  T 143.3 C; }
                { fraction 0.50;  T 204.4 C; }
                { fraction 0.70;  T 250.6 C; }
                { fraction 0.90;  T 291.7 C; }
            );
        }

        feed    { specificGravityFrom TBP; }

        cuts    { boundaries ( 0.0 0.3 0.7 1.0 ); }

        specificGravity { method constantWatsonK; }

        closure { specificGravityTolerance 0.005; }   // tighter than the miss, on purpose

        output  { proposal auto; prefix petCut3; file cuts3.csv; }
    }
);
